import SwiftUI
import KwipMLCore

enum GameRuleLessonContent {
    static let objective="This canvas lets you define and test the rules of a game before teaching a model to play. **Author game rules** contains a small take-away game: two players take turns removing one, two or three objects from a pile. The player who takes the last object wins.\n\nThe engine reads your state fields and rule expressions; it does not select a hidden implementation from the game's name. The two View blocks show complete rule-test results and each move's before/action/after state. No model or external file is needed."
    static let terms="- **State:** the values needed to describe the current position. Here, remaining is the pile size and to_move is player 0 or 1.\n- **Action:** a player's proposed move, such as take: 3.\n- **Legal rule:** the condition deciding whether that move is allowed.\n- **Transition:** the changes committed together after a legal move.\n- **Observation:** the numeric view a model would receive in the later learning stage.\n- **Reward:** an outcome value from a specified player's perspective."
    static let steps=[
        "Select [Author game rules](kwip-node://data/rules) and choose Edit rules. General shows the definition name and three editable examples. Stay with Take-away first. No model training or game visualization is involved. Each example uses the same rule evaluator.",
        "Open State. remaining starts at 21 and is bounded between 0 and 21; to_move starts at 0. Open Actions: take is an integer from 1 to 3. Bounds limit possible values; they do not alone decide whether a particular move is legal. Field edits update the draft after Update field. They do not change the saved canvas until Apply.",
        "Open Rules. legal checks action.take <= state.remaining, so a player cannot remove more objects than remain. The transition subtracts the chosen amount and switches to_move. Terminal rules recognize an empty pile and identify the previous player as winner. reward gives the winner +1, the other player −1, and zero before completion. Expressions are a bounded language, never general Python.",
        "Open Tests. Read Take 3 from 5, Too many objects, and Last object wins. Predict the resulting state for each. Validate & run tests uses a disposable isolated job: it does not run the rest of the canvas. Change an expected remaining value in a test, update the draft section and validate again. A failed test must remain a failure. Restore the correct expectation and validate before Apply.",
        "Apply, close the editor and Run Flow. Select [Inspect rule tests](kwip-node://data/tests), then [Inspect move trace](kwip-node://data/trace). Double-click before or after cells to inspect their full JSON. A refused move must preserve the previous state. History records outputs according to your capture settings; merely viewing rows never repeats a move or external write.",
        "Return to Edit rules → General. Try Load placement, then Load gravity in separate draft validations. The first uses cell coordinates; the second computes a landing position with generic array/range expressions. Inspect their observations, legal predicates and tests. Their game names do not change interpreter behavior. Choose an example, validate, Apply and Save when ready. The Run bounded game episodes practice connects this definition to a frozen policy; Learn from bounded self-play adds training. A visual lab is separate future work."
    ]
    static let boundary="This editable practice is unscored. Rule-authoring foundation only: two-player deterministic rules, finite integer/Boolean/enum actions, bounded typed state and numeric observation mappings. No model training, search, self-play or board renderer. Definition is an exact JSON/SHA-256 table output for inspection/export; Game session and Train Self-play consume this exact definition through their Definition inputs. JSON imports are data, not executable scripts. Invalid actions commit no state changes; resource limits and evaluator failures are distinct from wins/draws. Validate runs only this definition; Run Flow can execute other explicit downstream nodes, including writes, so review the canvas before running."
}
struct GameRuleLearningView:View {
    @ObservedObject var store:WorkspaceStore
    var body:some View {Button("Open game rule-authoring practice"){_=store.loadComponentWorkflowExample(GameRuleConfiguration.lessonID,device:"cpu")}.buttonStyle(FlatButtonStyle()).disabled(store.isBusy)}
}
